  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 340 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nNot much to speak on at least in the very near-term. Current \nsatellite showing a band of scattered upper-level cirrostratus along \nwith some patches of mid-level cumulus across portions of northern \ndown into western MN. Earlier today, this area of cumulus has been \ntrying to squeeze out a few droplets of precip however, they are \nrunning into dry air as noted in our 12z UA sounding. Perhaps some \nof you have noticed that there is a stark contrast between the hue \nof blue sky (or lack thereof) on the horizon as compared to \nyesterday at this time. This is a result of from upper-level flow \ncarrying in PacNorthwest wildfire smoke. Skies will have a bit of \nhaziness for at least the next 24 to 36 hours before a cold \nfront will push everything off to the southeast. Temperatures \nfor the remainder of today will reach near 80 degrees and \nperhaps may need to trend back a degree or two based off of some\nelevated smoke limiting \"some\" solar radiation's ability to \nheat the surface. It will feel a bit sticker too as compared to \nyesterday, dew points have crept back into the lower 60s as seen\non western MN surface observations. \n\nDew points will continue to trend up tonight into tomorrow thanks to \na warm advective regime out ahead of a parent troughing pattern\nwhich will span from Manitoba to Quebec. This trough features a\nsurface cold front that is expected to sweep through the \nnorthern plains from NW to SE through the course of Friday. \nThere has been some guidance that wants to produce precip for \nsouthwestern MN during the pre-dawn hours of Friday. However, \ndigging into forecast soundings, enough surface dry air appears \nto be in place which should suppress all if any moisture from \nreaching the surface which seems reasonably represented by the \nlatest NBM PoPs. The concern for strong storms starts late \nmorning to early afternoon across eastern MN into western WI. It\nis expect for CI to begin mainly near the I-35 corridor as \nearly as 11AM although more likely near 2-3PM. Mixed layer \ninstability nearing 2000 J/kg, combined with steep lapse rates \nbetween 7-8km supports a few stronger updrafts could develop \nwithin a broken line of storms. Thus the SPC has maintained \ntheir marginal risk for severe storms mainly for areas east of \nline from New Ulm, MN, up to the Twin Cities Metro, then up \nthrough Taylors Falls, WI. Primary threats will mainly be an \nisolated damaging wind gust up to 60 mph and large hail around \n1\" diameter. Rainfall amounts wont be too exciting with areas \nseeing a few hundredths to a couple tenths of a inch with \nlocally higher amounts limited to any stronger cells. \n\nAfter the cold front shoves off to our south and east Friday night, \nwe will receive a short break from the sticker conditions on \nSaturday. At least for the first half of the day, dew points fall \nback into the 50s with temperatures in the low to mid 80s for \nforecasted highs. The aforementioned troughing pattern will \nremain quasi-stationary over the Hudson Bay. This will\ntransition our upper- flow to more zonal in nature allowing for\nof an active weather pattern for the weekend. The next shot of \nrain chances comes Saturday night as a weak wave provides a \nglancing blow mainly along the I-90 corridor. The following wave\ncoming in from the Dakotas looks to have better localized \nforcing for us thus better chances of seeing rain/storms Sunday \nnight into Monday.",
